## What is proven

Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, the exact `is_sorted` body in
`solution.mpy` has the following partial-correctness property: for every finite
list of non-negative K `Int` values, if the call terminates, it returns `true`
exactly when the list is nondecreasing and no value occurs more than twice.

The proof does not merely invoke a result summary for the Python function.  It
loads the module, binds the exact closure, evaluates the argument, creates the
call frame, executes the initialization and first loop iteration, applies a
separately proved exact loop reachability theorem, and checks the return/pop
state.

## Formal claims

`SPEC.loop-invariant` starts at the real `#loop` state with the exact loop body,
singleton `Return(...) .Stmts` continuation, local bindings, module closure,
empty heap, call frame, and fixed builtin scope.  It proves the returned value
is `scanSorted(RESULT, PREV, DUPLICATES, IS)`.  This claim is proved against
`VERIFICATION`, where the entry-proof loop bridge is absent.

`SPEC.is-sorted` starts from `#loadAll(Module(FuncDef(...)))`, calls the loaded
`is_sorted` binding with `list(intVals(INPUT))`, requires
`nonNegativeInts(INPUT)`, and proves the returned value is
`scanSorted(true, -1, 0, INPUT)`.  It is proved against `VERIFICATION-ENTRY`,
which imports the independently established loop theorem as an exact
operational bridge.

The formal input representation is a finite `IntSeq`.  `intVals` denotes its
corresponding `ValSeq`; bare `list(ValSeq)` values are explicitly supported as
read-only claim inputs by the supplied semantics.

